---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../lib/site.js";
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Cookie Policy | CurlToCode"
  description="CurlToCode sets no cookies of its own. This page lists the advertising cookies Google may set, and explains how to refuse or change your choice."
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Cookie Policy"
  lede="We set no cookies ourselves. The only cookies this site can place come from the advertising that funds it, and you can refuse them."
  updated="2026-08-12"
  crumb="Cookie Policy"
  path="/cookies"
>
  <h2>Cookies we set</h2>
  <p>
    None. CurlToCode has no accounts, no sessions, and no server that could read
    a cookie, so it has no reason to set one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site does store a single value in your browser's local storage under the
    key <code>curltocode-theme</code>, holding your light, dark, or system
    appearance choice. Local storage is not a cookie: it is never attached to a
    network request and is only ever read by the page itself. It contains no
    identifier and cannot be used to recognise you.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies set by advertising</h2>
  <p>
    Advertising on this site is served by Google AdSense. When ads load, Google
    and its advertising partners may set cookies on your device. Broadly they
    fall into three groups:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Necessary for ad delivery</strong> — used to serve ads at all, to cap
      how often you see the same one, and to detect invalid traffic and fraud.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measurement</strong> — used to count impressions and report on how ads
      performed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Personalisation</strong> — used to select ads based on your activity
      on other sites. These are only used where the law allows, and in the EEA, the
      UK, and Switzerland only with your consent.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Google maintains the authoritative, current list of the specific cookies
    involved, which changes more often than this page would:
    <a
      href="https://business.safety.google/adscookies/"
      rel="noopener nofollow"
      target="_blank">Types of cookies used by Google</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    These cookies are set by Google, not by us. We cannot read them and we
    receive none of the data they collect.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <p>
    If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, or
    Switzerland, a consent message appears before any personalisation cookie is
    used. Refusing is a single click, exactly like accepting. If you refuse, you
    still see ads — they are simply chosen without reference to your browsing
    history.
  </p>
  <p>To change an answer you have already given, or to opt out more broadly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Clear this site's cookies in your browser. The consent message will appear
      again on your next visit.
    </li>
    <li>
      Turn off ad personalisation for your whole Google account at <a
        href="https://myadcenter.google.com"
        rel="noopener nofollow"
        target="_blank">My Ad Center</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      Block or delete cookies in your browser settings. Every major browser can
      block third-party cookies entirely; doing so does not stop the converter
      from working, because the converter does not use cookies.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Does refusing break anything?</h2>
  <p>
    No. Every feature of this site — conversion in both directions, all
    twenty-two generator targets, the request inspector, and the theme switcher
    — works identically whether you accept cookies or refuse them. The converter
    is plain JavaScript running on your machine and does not depend on any of
    this.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about cookies can go to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
      >{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
    >. Our <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> covers data handling more broadly.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
